:name: STM32F4 timer LED-cadence dynamic test
:description: Loads the timer example firmware under test and leaves the monitor open on a TCP port so an outside script can poll GPIOD's ODR register directly to observe (and time) LED toggling.

# Run this with:
#   renode --disable-gui -P 4568 timer_led_test.resc &
# Then, from another terminal:
#   python3 timer_led_probe.py
#
# -P 4568 (distinct from miniblink_test.resc's 4567, to allow running
# alongside a concurrent miniblink test without a port clash) puts Renode's
# own monitor console on a TCP port instead of an interactive prompt attached
# to this shell's stdin. --disable-gui skips the GUI entirely.
#
# Same GPIOD ODR polling technique as miniblink_test.resc/led_probe.py, but
# this test cares about TOGGLE RATE, not just toggle-vs-stuck: T4's seeded
# defect (TIM_DIER_UIE instead of TIM_DIER_CC1IE) is predicted to still
# toggle the LED, just at a ~13.1s period (65536 counts @ 5kHz update-event
# wrap) instead of the intended sub-second-to-few-second Morse cadence.

using sysbus
mach create "timer_led_test"

# stm32f4_discovery.repl ships with Renode itself (Antmicro's official
# platform description for the real ST STM32F4-Discovery board). It wires
# GPIOD pin 12 to a genuine Miscellaneous.LED model (UserLED), not a stub.
machine LoadPlatformDescription @platforms/boards/stm32f4_discovery.repl

# Overridable at the command line with -e '$elf=@/path/to/other.elf' or via
# --variable; defaults to the T4-defect-seeded build captured in /tmp.
$elf?=@/tmp/timer_T4_defect.elf

sysbus LoadELF $elf

# Actually starts CPU execution — without this, the ELF is loaded but the
# emulated core never runs, and GPIOD's ODR would never change either way.
start
